<div class="wrap cols"><article class="prose"><section class="snap"><h2>Covington at a glance</h2>
<dl class="snap-facts"><div><dt>County</dt><dd>Kenton County</dd></div><div><dt>Region</dt><dd>Northern Kentucky</dd></div><div><dt>Housing mix</dt><dd>Nineteenth-century brick row houses and Italianate homes dominate the older neighborhoods, with hillside homes in surrounding cities and newer construction farther south in Kenton County.</dd></div></dl>
<h3>Local claim risks</h3><ul class="tags"><li>Windstorm</li><li>Hail on slate and older roofs</li><li>Hillside landslides</li><li>Basement water and sewer backup</li><li>Freeze damage</li></ul>
<h3>Neighborhoods and nearby communities</h3><ul class="tags light"><li>MainStrasse Village</li><li>Licking Riverside</li><li>Latonia</li><li>Old Seminary Square</li><li>Wallace Woods</li><li>Austinburg</li><li>Park Hills</li><li>Fort Mitchell</li><li>Ludlow</li><li>Fort Wright</li></ul>
<h3>Weather history</h3><ol class="mini-tl"><li><b>1937</b><span>The Ohio River flood swamped low-lying Covington and led to the floodwall that protects the city today.</span></li><li><b>2008</b><span>Remnants of Hurricane Ike drove a September windstorm through the Cincinnati and Northern Kentucky area.</span></li><li><b>2012</b><span>The March 2 tornado outbreak struck parts of Northern Kentucky and the surrounding region.</span></li><li><b>2019</b><span>An exceptionally wet year triggered numerous landslides on hillsides across Northern Kentucky and Cincinnati.</span></li></ol></section>
<h2>Brick, Slate and Craftsmanship</h2>
<p>Walk through MainStrasse Village or Licking Riverside and you see the challenge: soft historic brick, slate and standing-seam roofs, cornices, tall windows and plaster interiors. When a storm damages these homes, the right repair is rarely the cheapest one in an estimating program. Historic district rules can also limit material choices, which affects cost.</p>
<p>The disputes here are often about <strong>like kind and quality</strong>. A slate roof is priced as asphalt. Old-growth trim is priced as finger-jointed pine. A plaster wall is priced as drywall with no allowance for the texture or thickness needed to blend with surrounding walls. Those choices add up quickly in a three-story row house.</p>
<h2>Hillsides and Earth Movement</h2>
<p>Northern Kentucky has some of the most landslide-prone terrain in the state. Colluvial soils over shale move when saturated, and wet years bring cracked foundations, leaning retaining walls and shifted driveways. It is important to be direct about coverage: <strong>earth movement and landslide are commonly excluded</strong> on standard homeowners policies unless you carry a specific endorsement.</p>
<p>Where it gets more nuanced is when a covered peril causes part of the damage. A burst pipe that saturates soil, or a falling tree that cracks a wall, can produce a covered loss even near unstable ground. Separating those causes takes careful documentation.</p>
<h2>Condos and Multi-Unit Buildings</h2>
<p>Covington has a growing number of condo conversions and small apartment buildings. These claims involve a master policy, unit owner policies and an association with its own deductible. Disagreements over who owes what for interior finishes are common. Our <a href="/insurance-appraisal/commercial-hoa-appraisal/">commercial and HOA appraisal</a> page explains how appraisal can apply to association claims.</p>
<ul>
<li>Shared roofs and party walls create allocation questions.</li>
<li>Older buildings often trigger code upgrades once walls are opened.</li>
<li>Matching exterior brick and mortar color across a whole facade.</li>
</ul>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Across the river</b>Many Covington contractors also work in Cincinnati. Pricing should reflect the local labor market, not national averages.</div>
<h2>Serving Kenton County On Site</h2>
<p>We inspect in Covington and across Kenton, Boone and Campbell counties. Each estimate includes measurements, photos and notes on historic materials, so the other appraiser can see exactly why a line item costs what it does. To understand how the full process works in Kentucky, see our overview of the <a href="/kentucky-appraisal-process/">Kentucky appraisal process</a>.</p>
<p>Because so many Covington homes share walls, roofs or drainage with their neighbors, we also pay attention to where one owner’s responsibility ends and another’s begins. Clear documentation of party walls, shared gutters and common roof sections keeps the estimate focused on the damage your policy actually covers and avoids disputes that stall a claim for months.</p>
<h2>Appraisal services available in Covington</h2><p>Every Kentucky Insurance Appraiser service is available to owners in Covington and across Kenton County, including:</p><ul><li><a href="/insurance-appraisal/party-appraiser-representation/">Appraisal Clause Representation</a></li><li><a href="/insurance-appraisal/home-insurance-appraisal/">Homeowners Insurance Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/insurance-appraisal/commercial-hoa-appraisal/">Commercial &amp; HOA Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/insurance-appraisal/roof-hail-appraisal/">Roof &amp; Hail Damage Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/insurance-appraisal/tornado-wind-appraisal/">Tornado &amp; Wind Damage Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/insurance-appraisal/water-damage-appraisal/">Water Damage Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/insurance-appraisal/fire-smoke-appraisal/">Fire &amp; Smoke Damage Appraisal</a></li></ul>
<h2>Covington claim questions</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Is landslide damage covered in Covington?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><p>Usually not under a standard homeowners policy, since earth movement is commonly excluded. Some insurers sell endorsements, and damage from a separate covered cause may still be payable.</p></details><details><summary>Can appraisal force the insurer to pay for slate instead of asphalt?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><p>Appraisal sets the cost to repair with like kind and quality. If the roof was slate, a properly supported estimate should price slate, and the umpire can decide disputed items.</p></details><details><summary>Do you handle condo association claims in Northern Kentucky?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><p>Yes. We work with associations and individual unit owners on master policy and unit policy disputes.</p></details></div>
